  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What heats the water in a hot spring?",
      "a": "Heat from inside the Earth. In volcanic places, magma warms the rock near the surface; elsewhere, water simply sinks deep enough to reach warm rock, since the Earth gets about 25 degrees hotter for every kilometre down.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the Earth heat water?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does hot spring water come from?",
      "a": "It is rain and melted snow that soaked into the ground, was heated deep underground, and rose back up through cracks. It can spend years to thousands of years underground.",
      "asks": [
        "Is hot spring water rainwater?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do some hot springs smell of eggs?",
      "a": "Some hot water carries hydrogen sulfide gas from volcanic rock. This gas smells like rotten eggs.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do volcanic springs smell?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are some hot springs brightly coloured?",
      "a": "Tiny living things called microbes live in the hot water. Different kinds grow at different temperatures and colour the water and rock blue, green, yellow and orange, as at the Grand Prismatic Spring in Yellowstone.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes hot springs colourful?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is it safe to swim in a hot spring?",
      "a": "Only in springs made safe for bathing. Some natural springs are boiling hot or acidic and can cause terrible burns, so always follow the signs.",
      "asks": [
        "Can you bathe in any hot spring?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "On average the Earth's crust gets about 25 to 30 degrees Celsius hotter with every kilometre of depth.",
    "Iceland uses geothermal hot water to heat most of its homes."
